nsure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342406/field-3/b078929a2c7936cc58936f9a3dc3ad9d34bf096fcbea293ffdde0acd0defc39d" :answer/id #uuid "4a5b8564-2c8e-571f-a507-f88dd03e0c97" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "True" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342406/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "44c08949-d303-5df6-9ab0-e3921c78db56" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342406/field-3/ef8a69cb660449e64835b31d26955ef0433e1eb49332071bb2f143d8ceea2652" :answer-field/choices ["q-342406/field-3/ef8a69cb660449e64835b31d26955ef0433e1eb49332071bb2f143d8ceea2652" "q-342406/field-3/b078929a2c7936cc58936f9a3dc3ad9d34bf096fcbea293ffdde0acd0defc39d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342406/field-4/b078929a2c7936cc58936f9a3dc3ad9d34bf096fcbea293ffdde0acd0defc39d" :answer/id #uuid "7f38b75c-9886-5bfb-bc62-878870ba47f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "True" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342406/field-4/ef8a69cb660449e64835b31d26955ef0433e1eb49332071bb2f143d8ceea2652" :answer/id #uuid "95472977-2896-52fe-92b0-4c3e64d6178f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "False" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342406/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "7a187c9e-0e47-503e-bce4-9a2fb221d2aa" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342406/field-4/ef8a69cb660449e64835b31d26955ef0433e1eb49332071bb2f143d8ceea2652" :answer-field/choices ["q-342406/field-4/b078929a2c7936cc58936f9a3dc3ad9d34bf096fcbea293ffdde0acd0defc39d" "q-342406/field-4/ef8a69cb660449e64835b31d26955ef0433e1eb49332071bb2f143d8ceea2652"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342406" :question/id #uuid "0295482c-764c-562f-9c52-522e737fa963" :question/math-academy-id "q-342406" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ -2, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/7cfd6da222fb36d06be1153ac0e398e567f8b9fb8cd1cd9e21f1bb35ed131f8d.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}(x+1)=-2+1=-1\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-2)=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and III are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/a59fad69c9790a2b899c21fd730f779c28bc9b70f44a67851ad5ca92bff21951.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342406/field-1" "q-342406/field-2" "q-342406/field-3" "q-342406/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ae32a2a-438f-547c-8510-7e9f760c3cb6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342406"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342657/field-1/b078929a2c7936cc58936f9a3dc3ad9d34bf096fcbea293ffdde0acd0defc39d" :answer/id #uuid "2ce32888-42df-