:answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343339/field-3/c2dda5a7dbce757bff6664cca53792d1d9028f01cf844b142bd73cf1554eac87" :answer/id #uuid "488fa02c-f39b-5562-9af4-4d43d2dcafb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343339/field-3/4452e375b6e64616fb4c7dd3a3f3f160f721243606ad695c99ffafb7b1e35936" :answer/id #uuid "53d68deb-57a2-5a08-885c-2adbdcbfd5c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343339/field-3/d8acb7a23a3e5e16b0c452860c4cdb5d2b72c8a9a46f96db3f130bd65cc9d382" :answer/id #uuid "0e00fdb5-855c-53d0-a362-2c49942d2654" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343339/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "75977523-5501-5aa4-a172-8b2715e16225" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-343339/field-3/c2dda5a7dbce757bff6664cca53792d1d9028f01cf844b142bd73cf1554eac87" :answer-field/choices ["q-343339/field-3/44e8affcc126e6a0157c5f2eea38a5770f68f559a164baa3f2e02216359b4229" "q-343339/field-3/c2dda5a7dbce757bff6664cca53792d1d9028f01cf844b142bd73cf1554eac87" "q-343339/field-3/4452e375b6e64616fb4c7dd3a3f3f160f721243606ad695c99ffafb7b1e35936" "q-343339/field-3/d8acb7a23a3e5e16b0c452860c4cdb5d2b72c8a9a46f96db3f130bd65cc9d382"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-343339/field-4/1c80dacf3d3de89bdd920d02fb821f59c41fe0917b6b080760e77402555b17d2" :answer/id #uuid "17e47812-8047-5b3e-b667-e4bea251a9c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343339/field-4/332947acfd5b898fb44e53575ac4256d70d709211ae790bca49622de5802082c" :answer/id #uuid "bc61008f-80f5-501a-b6ee-f64e941d77d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343339/field-4/2d7bd71f08af182db17695447d773283f8679f955220bde57689fc8bbf9db5c3" :answer/id #uuid "c211b2ba-88b4-5cd9-9391-2945e9a3381c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343339/field-4/e7988892539e8db1c8ce4a26463c3d73bb2b7f958ca8ac935e9dcb55b315e9e5" :answer/id #uuid "90681fa5-7d9a-59e9-b3f4-3f7958d8a7f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343339/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "91140984-2cd4-551a-beb9-61580d98c75a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-343339/field-4/332947acfd5b898fb44e53575ac4256d70d709211ae790bca49622de5802082c" :answer-field/choices ["q-343339/field-4/1c80dacf3d3de89bdd920d02fb821f59c41fe0917b6b080760e77402555b17d2" "q-343339/field-4/332947acfd5b898fb44e53575ac4256d70d709211ae790bca49622de5802082c" "q-343339/field-4/2d7bd71f08af182db17695447d773283f8679f955220bde57689fc8bbf9db5c3" "q-343339/field-4/e7988892539e8db1c8ce4a26463c3d73bb2b7f958ca8ac935e9dcb55b315e9e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-343339" :question/id #uuid "4467f0cd-7b61-5702-b8db-33e570ded622" :question/math-academy-id "q-343339" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $2$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-4x$ defined on $[-1,1).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $2$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=-2{x}^{2}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-4x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=1$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-1,1).$ \nTherefore, the $2$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-4t)\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ [-2{t}^{2}]_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi t)]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -2{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-343339/field-1" "q-343339/field-2" "q-343339/field-3" "q-343339/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e01d04-6c13-52eb-a4b6-ad9a5cde8134"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-343339"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21846"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/9b34c017e88b9ed4f80f45223c5093a2f934d249d666b3c1d929718bc05e9882.png)\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x \\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\nDetermine whether each statement below is true or false.\n\n- $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in(-2,2).$\n\n- $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in(-2,2).$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x).$\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $\\mathbb{R}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x \\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/5c1cc57825a318df088ac0bc28ffe75c21579c5fe007fbb52cf0710a1937dc1e.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x \\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} (-2x) = -2(-2) = 4\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} x^2 = (2)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is true since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/f5fb59869184a0c54ddaeea0b434104f3335c652c3f7533cd9dec0dcdcc91876.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21845"] :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $10$-periodic extension of the function\n$F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3$\ndefined on $[-5,5).$ Fill in the blanks in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension of $G(x)=5x$\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/178be718fdba262f895193f9c503667f4ba3f709db23717893016d500ba4bc8b.png)\nSince $f$ is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/6c274adc750119956ce950c5212283e01dfddf37137178bf0b85295a6837f433.png)\nand  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/6c274adc750119956ce950c5212283e01dfddf37137178bf0b85295a6837f433.png)\non $\\mathbb{R},$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/9d14fd1a227fc073b09d78684cb8dbff507bf083da988dda2755270a77981455.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=5.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{107}{6}\n+ \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{250(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^2}\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that  \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-5,5),$\n\n- continuous on $[-5,5),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-5)=F(5),$ if we extend the value at $x=5$ using the formula $F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3.$\n\nTherefore, the $10$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{continuous} }$ and $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise smooth}}$\non $\\mathbb{R},$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-3) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{107}{6})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-50(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}) \\\\ 5x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21856"] :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $8$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-\\dfrac{3}{4}x^2$ defined on $[-4,4).$ Fill in the blanks in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $8$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{4}x^3 = \\int_0^x f(t) \\, \\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/7ddcc5e5d8580428341aec84ff2982c593054ec7ec363cc11139d87912dab58d.png)\n Since $f$ is \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/91f9e216819e2259c6cc4ce19c39e38f8d75c9a5902019de9e437e0732477943.png)\non $\\mathbb{R},$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/1c6cf5957b07d2564607d603276b7fb9cffbe7502f53c6ea77a10b726d95aefd.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n \\geq 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim -4+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{n^2\\pi^2}\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4).$ \nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise continuous}}$ on $\\mathbb{R},$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4}))\\text{d}t \\\\ {[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼{[-4t]}_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{4}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3} & ∼-4x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{192(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
